This project implements an RSA oracle that is vulnerable to side channel attacks like timing attacks, and implements a basic timing utility
that takes a directory of ciphertexts to gather decryptions times from.

------code files--------

keygen.cpp: generates secure, random public and private keyfiles that work with the oracle.

rsa.cpp: loads public or private keys from a keyfile and performs the encryptions / decryption operations. I simply used GMP's mpz_powm function (which is
based on square and multiply but optimized) to compute exponentiation for encryption, but I implemented square and multiply directly for decryption since I think mpz_powm optimizations could muddy timing results.

oracle.cpp: creates a decryption oracle server that runs over unix sockets. Idea is to avoid having overhead from starting the process over and over again,
this allows timing to be closer to just the square and multiply computation.
    - potential issue: currently reading in byte at a time, not efficient at all and I think we can stick to some constant size for inputs and make this better by reading all at once

cli.cpp: exposes encrypt and decrypt functions from rsa.cpp, useful for generating a bunch of test ciphertext

timing.cpp: takes a directory of ciphertexts to test with, runs them through the oracle (currently) 1000 times and gathers timing results for each iteration.
I think we may benefit from having a higher number of sample ciphertexts (~2000) and stick with 1000 iterations per sample. Puts results in csv file.

-----Testing the oracle with socat------

socat - UNIX-CONNECT:/tmp/rsa_oracle.sock

--------Running oracle----------------

I have been running this with core affinity to help with noise reduction:
taskset -c 0 ./oracle private.key
taskset -c 1 ./timing some-directory-of-ciphertext-samples

